 Storage: sites_add_storage provisions D1 (SQLite SQL via env.DB) or R2 (objects via env.FILES), isolated by preview/production. These are Workers bindings, not browser SDKs or PostgreSQL connections. Query them on the server; use bound SQL parameters. Initialize/migrate the D1 schema through controlled server-side code and test with local Wrangler D1/R2 first; there is no arbitrary SQL-write tool. Deploy after provisioning to attach bindings. Use D1/R2 for persistent user data; isolate-local Maps and arrays are disposable demo state.
 
 Realtime: for multiplayer, live updates, collaboration or presence, call sites_prepare_realtime and read its returned README. It creates local server/browser helper modules and a local Wrangler helper Worker without changing existing files or configs. Add realtime:true to the deployment manifest (Next.js: sites_prepare_next with realtime:true); Sites provides an isolated env.REALTIME Durable Object binding per environment. Implement a same-origin HTTP ticket route that authenticates with @freebuff/auth, checks room membership and derives userId from user.sub; the server chooses publish/presence capabilities, never the browser. Connect using the returned websocketUrl; the dispatcher upgrades WebSockets outside the application/Next route. Tickets are single-use, expire after 30 seconds and sockets reconnect with fresh authorization after at most 5 minutes. Realtime broadcasts/presence are ephemeral; store authoritative data in D1/R2, publish after successful writes, reload snapshots on every ready event, and reconcile revisions. Client-published events are untrusted; keep critical state and game rules in authenticated server mutations. Throttle interactive events instead of heartbeat loops. Start the helper and app in separate local Wrangler dev processes as the README describes (helper first, distinct inspector ports); a multi-config command exposes only the app's port. Test two clients, including unauthorized rooms and reconnect recovery. Cloud VM preview must expose the helper port and explicitly map its WebSocket URL; browser localhost does not reach the VM. Realtime currently uses the canonical Sites origin; test custom-domain navigation deliberately. Never expose platform Cloudflare credentials or upload the development helper as tenant code.
 
 Auth: when login/accounts/per-user data are requested, use sites_enable_auth${runtime === 'desktop' ? " with the local Worker's loopback origin" : ''}, then install @freebuff/auth and read node_modules/@freebuff/auth/AGENTS.md. If the package or issuer is unavailable, report the setup blocker; never invent credentials. ${runtime === 'cloud' ? 'VM loopback addresses are not reachable by the user’s browser: omit localOrigin for hosted auth and verify browser sign-in on the deployed preview origin. ' : ''}Its fetch SDK works in Workers/Hono (also Bun/Deno); Next.js apps use its documented Next.js adapter with getCloudflareContext from @opennextjs/cloudflare for runtime bindings; sites_prepare_next makes the resulting app deployable. A browser-only/static app needs a server Worker for auth. Wrap the handler once per isolate before API/static fallbacks using createFreebuffAuth({ clientId: env.FREEBUFF_CLIENT_ID, issuer: env.FREEBUFF_ISSUER, baseUrl: env.APP_ORIGIN }). These reserved public variables are injected on deploy; use returned development.variables locally, with allowInsecureLocalhost only for loopback development. Never derive baseUrl from a request Host. Preview and production share the OIDC production client with exact callbacks; localhost has a separate development client. No client secret is needed.
 
